   "i": 71,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Intensionalism and Supervenience together entail naturalism. Consider the moral property of wrongness. Every wrong action has some natural features; call the features of a given wrong action ⟦M⟧ . Supervenience entails that any action with ⟦M⟧ is necessarily wrong. Of course, there is more than one action that is wrong, but the argument generalizes. Call the natural features of a second wrong action ⟦M⟧ . Supervenience entails that any action with ⟦M⟧ is necessarily wrong. And so on, for all nat",
